\title{Finite Scaled Dot-Product Attention:\\Stochasticity, Permutation Equivariance, and Compositional Closure}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 3, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Scaled dot-product attention transforms a finite collection of query, key, and value vectors by exponentiating pairwise query--key scores, normalizing each query row, and averaging the values. We prove that every softmax denominator and weight is positive, every attention row sums to one, and arbitrary simultaneous relabeling of tokens transports the complete output exactly. We further prove preservation of tokenwise constant values and closure of equivariance under composition. Algorithms and numerical diagnostics are given, together with applications and a precise account of the additional hypotheses needed for universality, reproducing-kernel, masking, and multi-head rank claims.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Attention maps a collection of representations to a new collection by data-dependent averaging. It computes all query--key inner products, divides by a scale, applies row-wise softmax, and uses the resulting weights to combine value vectors. This compact formula encodes a probabilistic structure and an exact symmetry.

Exponentiation and normalization make each row of the attention matrix a probability vector. Because pairwise scores and finite sums carry no dependence on arbitrary token names, simultaneous relabeling of all token-indexed inputs merely relabels the output. Since the output retains one vector per token, this property is equivariance rather than invariance. Equivariance is stable under composition, allowing the local result to propagate through compatible layered architectures.

We work over arbitrary finite index sets. This emphasizes that token labels are representational rather than mathematical data. Query and key features share one finite coordinate set, while values may have another. Our results require no genericity, distinctness, or probabilistic assumptions on the vectors.

\section{Finite attention}
Let $I$ be a finite, nonempty token set, $D$ a finite query--key feature set, and $E$ a value feature set. For $i\in I$, let
\[
q_i,k_i:D\to\mathbb R,\qquad v_i:E\to\mathbb R.
\]
Let $s\ne0$ be a scale; conventionally $s=\sqrt{|D|}$.

\begin{definition}[Scaled score]
For $i,j\in I$, define
\[
S_{ij}=\frac{\langle q_i,k_j\rangle}{s}
=\frac1s\sum_{a\in D}q_i(a)k_j(a).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Softmax denominator and weight]
For $i,j\in I$, define
\[
Z_i=\sum_{\ell\in I}e^{S_{i\ell}},\qquad
W_{ij}=\frac{e^{S_{ij}}}{Z_i}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Scaled dot-product attention]
The attention output is
\[
A(q,k,v)_i(b)=\sum_{j\in I}W_{ij}v_j(b),\qquad b\in E.
\]
Equivalently, after choosing matrix representations,
\[
A(Q,K,V)=\operatorname{softmax}_{\rm row}(QK^{\mathsf T}/s)V.
\]
\end{definition}

\section{Probabilistic structure}
\begin{theorem}[Positive denominators]
For every $i\in I$, one has $Z_i>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every exponential $e^{S_{ij}}$ is strictly positive. Since $I$ is nonempty, $Z_i$ is a finite sum containing at least one positive term.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Positive weights]
For all $i,j\in I$, one has $W_{ij}>0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Both the exponential numerator and, by the preceding theorem, the denominator are positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Row normalization]
For every $i\in I$,
\[
\sum_{j\in I}W_{ij}=1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Using $Z_i>0$,
\[
\sum_jW_{ij}=\frac{\sum_j e^{S_{ij}}}{Z_i}=\frac{Z_i}{Z_i}=1.
\]
\end{proof}

Thus $W$ is a strictly positive row-stochastic matrix. For fixed queries and keys, attention is a Markov averaging operator acting independently on every value coordinate.

\begin{proposition}[Coordinatewise range bound]
For every $i\in I$ and $b\in E$,
\[
\min_jv_j(b)\le A(q,k,v)_i(b)\le\max_jv_j(b).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The output coordinate is a convex combination: its coefficients are positive and sum to one. Multiply the lower and upper bounds for each $v_j(b)$ by $W_{ij}$ and sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Convex-hull containment]
For every token $i$, the vector $A(q,k,v)_i$ belongs to the convex hull of $\{v_j:j\in I\}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The defining expression is a convex combination with coefficients $W_{ij}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Permutation equivariance}
Let $\sigma:I\to I$ be a permutation. For a token-indexed family $x$, define
\[
(\sigma x)_r=x_{\sigma^{-1}(r)}.
\]
Thus content formerly at $i$ moves to $\sigma(i)$.

\begin{definition}[Permutation equivariance]
A token-to-token map $F$ is equivariant under $\sigma$ if
\[
F(\sigma x)=\sigma F(x)
\]
for every input $x$. Full permutation equivariance means this holds for every permutation of $I$.
\end{definition}

Equivariance differs from invariance. An invariant map erases the effect of relabeling, while an equivariant map retains token-indexed outputs and transports them with the inputs.

Let $q'=\sigma q$ and $k'=\sigma k$. Then
\[
S'_{\sigma(i),\sigma(j)}
=\frac{\langle q'_{\sigma(i)},k'_{\sigma(j)}\rangle}{s}
=\frac{\langle q_i,k_j\rangle}{s}=S_{ij}.
\]
Moreover, by the change of variable $r=\sigma(j)$,
\[
Z'_{\sigma(i)}
=\sum_{r\in I}e^{S'_{\sigma(i),r}}
=\sum_{j\in I}e^{S'_{\sigma(i),\sigma(j)}}
=Z_i.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Attention-weight transport]
Under simultaneous permutation of queries and keys,
\[
W'_{\sigma(i),\sigma(j)}=W_{ij}
\]
for every $i,j\in I$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The transported score identity gives equal exponential numerators, while reindexing the finite denominator sum gives equal denominators.
\end{proof}

If $P$ is the permutation matrix associated with $\sigma$, this identity is $W'=PWP^{\mathsf T}$.

\begin{theorem}[Permutation Equivariance of Scaled Dot-Product Attention]
For every permutation $\sigma$ of $I$,
\[
A(\sigma q,\sigma k,\sigma v)=\sigma A(q,k,v).
\]
Equivalently,
\[
A(\sigma q,\sigma k,\sigma v)_{\sigma(i)}(b)
=A(q,k,v)_i(b)
\]
for all $i\in I$ and $b\in E$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $q'=\sigma q$, $k'=\sigma k$, and $v'=\sigma v$. Reindexing by $r=\sigma(j)$ and using weight transport gives
\begin{align*}
A(q',k',v')_{\sigma(i)}(b)
&=\sum_{r\in I}W'_{\sigma(i),r}v'_r(b)\\
&=\sum_{j\in I}W'_{\sigma(i),\sigma(j)}v'_{\sigma(j)}(b)\\
&=\sum_{j\in I}W_{ij}v_j(b)
=A(q,k,v)_i(b).
\end{align*}
\end{proof}

The theorem is exact and covers repeated tokens, tied or negative scores, and arbitrary finite feature dimensions.

\section{Preservation and composition}
\begin{theorem}[Preservation of tokenwise constants]
Suppose $v_j=c$ for every $j\in I$, where $c:E\to\mathbb R$. Then $A(q,k,v)_i=c$ for every $i\in I$, independently of $q$ and $k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For every coordinate $b$,
\[
A(q,k,v)_i(b)=\sum_jW_{ij}c(b)
=c(b)\sum_jW_{ij}=c(b).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Equivariant composition]
If token maps $F$ and $G$ are equivariant under $\sigma$, then $F\circ G$ is equivariant under $\sigma$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For every $x$,
\[
(F\circ G)(\sigma x)=F(G(\sigma x))
=F(\sigma G(x))=\sigma F(G(x))=\sigma(F\circ G)(x).
\]
\end{proof}

By induction, every finite composition of maps equivariant under the same permutations remains equivariant. Residual addition also preserves the property, since $\sigma x+F(\sigma x)=\sigma(x+F(x))$. A pointwise map applying the same function at every token is likewise equivariant. A complete block inherits the symmetry only when every component, including masks, normalization, and positional data, is compatible with it.

\section{Algorithms and diagnostics}
Direct exponentiation can overflow. For each score row let $m_i=\max_jS_{ij}$ and compute
\[
\widetilde W_{ij}
=\frac{e^{S_{ij}-m_i}}{\sum_\ell e^{S_{i\ell}-m_i}}.
\]
The common factor $e^{-m_i}$ cancels, so $\widetilde W=W$.

\begin{algorithm}[Stable scaled dot-product attention]
Given $Q,K,V$ and nonzero scale $s$: (1) validate dimensions; (2) compute $S=QK^{\mathsf T}/s$; (3) subtract each row maximum; (4) exponentiate; (5) divide every row by its sum to obtain $W$; and (6) return $WV$. For $n$ tokens, query--key dimension $d$, and value dimension $p$, the arithmetic cost is $O(n^2d+n^2p)$ and dense auxiliary storage is $O(n^2)$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Permutation-equivariance residual]
Compute $Y=A(Q,K,V)$. Choose a permutation index array $p$, compute $Y'=A(Q[p],K[p],V[p])$, and report
\[
r_{\rm eq}=\lVert Y'-Y[p]\rVert_{\max}.
\]
In exact arithmetic this residual is zero; in floating-point arithmetic it should be near rounding precision. Row normalization and constant preservation can similarly be diagnosed by
\[
r_{\rm row}=\max_i\left|\sum_jW_{ij}-1\right|,
\quad
r_{\rm const}=\lVert A(Q,K,\mathbf1c^{\mathsf T})-\mathbf1c^{\mathsf T}\rVert_{\max}.
\]
\end{algorithm}

Numerical tests illustrate the identities but are not their foundation. Stabilization leaves the mathematical weights unchanged, while floating-point summation order may create tiny nonzero residuals.

\section{Applications}
\paragraph{Set-valued data.}
Point clouds, object collections, particle systems, and many graph neighborhoods have no canonical enumeration. Equivariance prevents row order from becoming a spurious feature. An invariant pooling operation can follow an equivariant stack when a single global output is desired.

\paragraph{Molecules and physics.}
Atom indices are labels rather than observables. Relabeling atoms should relabel atomwise outputs and preserve global observables. Token symmetry addresses index permutations, though physical systems may additionally require rotational, translational, or reflection symmetry.

\paragraph{Language.}
Bare attention is fully permutation equivariant and cannot distinguish inputs differing only by a simultaneous reordering. Positional encodings intentionally augment content with order information. Causal masks further restrict access and therefore reduce the symmetry group.

\paragraph{Distributed sensing.}
Database identifiers may be arbitrary while coordinates and modalities are meaningful features. Equivariance ignores administrative reindexing while still responding to features carried with each sensor.

\section{Scope and limitations}
\subsection{Universality}
Permutation equivariance proves symmetry preservation, not density in all continuous equivariant maps. A universal approximation theorem must specify a compact permutation-invariant domain, an architecture class, activations, widths or depths, an output representation, a topology, an approximation norm, and a constructive density argument.

\subsection{Exponential kernels}
For
\[
K(x,y)=\exp\!\left(\frac{q(x)^{\mathsf T}k(y)}s\right),
\]
unrelated $q$ and $k$ generally give $K(x,y)\ne K(y,x)$. An unconditional reproducing-kernel conclusion is therefore unavailable. In the symmetric specialization $q=k=\phi$ with $s>0$,
\[
K(x,y)=\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{\langle\phi(x),\phi(y)\rangle^m}{m!s^m}
\]
suggests a positive-definite feature map through symmetric tensor powers. Constructing the associated Hilbert space is a separate result.

\subsection{Multi-head rank}
Additional heads do not automatically increase rank: duplicate heads are a counterexample. An additive rank theorem requires independence, for example that a new head's column space intersects the existing concatenated column space only at zero. Under such a direct-sum hypothesis, dimensions add.

\subsection{Masks}
A mask changes the keys available to each query. Equivariance survives for permutations preserving the mask relation. A causal mask is not preserved by arbitrary token permutations, so its symmetry group is smaller.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The dependency chain is short and informative. Positivity of the exponential yields positive denominators; normalization yields stochastic rows; reindexing a finite sum yields weight transport; a second reindexing yields output equivariance; row normalization yields constant preservation; and substitution yields compositional closure.

Multi-head attention should next be treated as parallel equivariant heads followed by concatenation and tokenwise output projection, with rank claims separated and supplied with independence assumptions. Masks should be formulated relationally and analyzed through their automorphism groups. Stochasticity should be used to derive norm, convex-hull, and Lipschitz bounds. The symmetric exponential dot-product kernel invites a power-series feature-space construction, whereas asymmetric query--key similarities require a two-sided interpretation. Approximation theory must combine the present symmetry statement with a precise constructive density theorem. Finally, pointwise feed-forward layers, residual paths, and compatible normalization can be assembled into a complete equivariant encoder stack by repeated use of compositional closure.

\section{Conclusion}
Finite scaled dot-product softmax attention has exact structural guarantees: weights are strictly positive, rows sum to one, simultaneous token relabeling transports weights and outputs exactly, constant values are preserved, and equivariance survives composition. These facts make attention naturally suited to collections with arbitrary labels and identify precisely where positions, masks, and geometry must enter when additional structure matters. Stronger universality, kernel, and rank statements remain possible directions, but require hypotheses beyond the foundational symmetry and normalization laws established here.

